            0.149
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) How many matchsticks will be required for the 15th stage of the pattern?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.154,
            0.886,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(v) Can 200 matchsticks form a stage in this pattern? Justify your answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.203,
            0.885,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*13. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x ) = a x + b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "q(x) = cx + d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be two linear polynomials such that:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.25,
            0.883,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) The graph of p(x) passes through the points (2, 3) and (6, 11)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.276,
            0.772,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) The graph of q(x) passes through the point (4, –1)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.302,
            0.754,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) The graph of q(x) is parallel to the graph of p(x)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.332,
            0.886,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Find the polynomials p(x) and q(x). Also, find the coordinates of the point where these lines meet the x-axis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.381,
            0.885,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*14. What do all linear functions of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f(x)=ax+a,a>0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , have in common?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.37,
            0.452,
            0.629,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.495,
            0.886,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z An "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "algebraic expression",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " combines numbers, variables, and operation symbols. For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x^{2} + 5xy - 3y^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an algebraic expression in the variables x and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar{y} \\cdot 2x^{2},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " 5xy and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "- 3 y ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "terms",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the algebraic expression, and the numbers 2, 5 and – 3 are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "coefficients",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the terms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.606,
            0.886,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Univariate Polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are algebraic expressions in one variable. Thus "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2} + 5x \\mp 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 y ^ { 3 } \\; - \\; 4 y ^ { 2 } \\; + \\; 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are univariate polynomials in x and y, respectively. The highest power of the variable in a univariate polynomial is called its "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "degree",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Thus, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 } + 5 x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is of degree 2 while "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 y ^ { 3 } - 4 y ^ { 2 } + 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is of degree 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.716,
            0.885,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z A polynomial of degree one is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear polynomial",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Hence, 2x + 3 and 5 – 4y are linear polynomials in the variables x and y, respectively."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.787,
            0.886,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Linear growth",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " refers to a pattern in which a quantity increases by a fixed amount over equal intervals. In contrast, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear decay",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " describes a pattern in which a quantity decreases by a fixed amount over equal intervals."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
